{"prompt": {"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant that repairs Java 11 code so that it correctly solves the given programming problem. The code reads and writes I/O from the console."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Repair the following Java 11 code so that it correctly solves the programming problem. The code reads and writes I/O from the console. Your repaired code should implement the problem correctly, meaning that it should produce the correct output for any valid input.\nPrint your repaired code and also the confidence score you give to your repair. The confidence score should be a number between 0 and 1. The format of the output should be:\n<repaired_code>\n<your_repaired_code>\n</repaired_code>\n<confidence>\n<your_confidence_score>\n</confidence>\nDo not provide any other text or markdown fences.\n\nHere is the problem description:\n<problem_description>\nLittle Johnny Bubbles enjoys spending hours in front of his computer playing video games. His favorite game is Bubble Strike, fast-paced bubble shooting online game for two players.Each game is set in one of the N maps, each having different terrain configuration. First phase of each game decides on which map the game will be played. The game system randomly selects three maps and shows them to the players. Each player must pick one of those three maps to be discarded. The game system then randomly selects one of the maps that were not picked by any of the players and starts the game.Johnny is deeply enthusiastic about the game and wants to spend some time studying maps, thus increasing chances to win games played on those maps. However, he also needs to do his homework, so he does not have time to study all the maps. That is why he asked himself the following question: \"What is the minimum number of maps I have to study, so that the probability to play one of those maps is at least $$$P$$$\"?Can you help Johnny find the answer for this question? You can assume Johnny's opponents do not know him, and they will randomly pick maps.\n</problem_description>\n\nHere is the Java 11 code you have to repair:\n<original_code>\nimport java.util.*;\nimport java.io.*;\n\npublic class _1599_C {\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {\n        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);\n        PrintWriter out = new PrintWriter(System.out);\n        int n = sc.nextInt();\n        double p = sc.nextDouble();\n        sc.nextLine(); // consume newline\n        int total = n < 3 ? 0 : n * (n - 1) * (n - 2) / 6;\n        for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {\n            double prob = 0.0;\n            int ni = n - i;\n            if (ni >= 2) prob += (double) i * ni * (ni - 1) / 2 / total * 0.5;\n            if (i >= 2) prob += (double) (i * (i - 1) / 2) * ni / total;\n            if (i >= 3) prob += (double) (i * (i - 1) * (i - 2) / 6) / total;\n            if (prob >= p) {\n                out.println(i);\n                break;\n            }\n        }\n        sc.close();\n        out.close();\n    }\n}\n</original_code>\n"}], "temp": 0, "sample_size": 1, "logprobs": true, "model": "qwen/qwen3-coder-next"}, "response": {"samples": [{"content": "<repaired_code>\nimport java.util.*;\nimport java.io.*;\n\npublic class _1599_C {\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {\n        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);\n        PrintWriter out = new PrintWriter(System.out);\n        int n = sc.nextInt();\n        double p = sc.nextDouble();\n        sc.nextLine(); // consume newline\n        \n        // Handle edge case when n < 3\n        if (n < 3) {\n            out.println(0);\n            sc.close();\n            out.close();\n            return;\n        }\n        \n        int total = n * (n - 1) * (n - 2) / 6;\n        \n        for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {\n            double prob = 0.0;\n            \n            // Case 1: Johnny studies i maps, opponent picks randomly\n            // We need to calculate probability that the final map is one Johnny studied\n            \n            // Case 1: All 3 selected maps are from Johnny's studied maps (i maps)\n            // Probability: C(i,3)/C(n,3) * 1 (since all 3 are studied, Johnny will pick one to discard, \n            // but the remaining 2 are both studied, and system picks randomly among non-discarded)\n            // Actually, if all 3 are studied, Johnny will discard one, opponent discards one (randomly),\n            // and the remaining map is studied. So probability = 1 in this case.\n            if (i >= 3) {\n                prob += (double) (i * (i - 1) * (i - 2) / 6) / total;\n            }\n            \n            // Case 2: Exactly 2 maps are from Johnny's studied maps, 1 is not\n            // Number of ways: C(i,2) * C(n-i,1)\n            // In this case: Johnny will keep one of the studied maps (he wants to win on studied maps)\n            // Opponent picks randomly among 3 maps\n            // We need to compute probability that the final map is studied\n            \n            // Johnny's strategy: discard the un-studied map if present\n            // Opponent: random discard\n            \n            // If Johnny sees 2 studied + 1 un-studied:\n            // Johnny will discard the un-studied one\n            // Opponent randomly picks one of 3 to discard\n            //   - If opponent discards un-studied (prob 1/3): remaining 2 are studied, system picks randomly -> prob 1 to get studied\n            //   - If opponent discards studied1 (prob 1/3): remaining are studied2 and un-studied, system picks randomly -> prob 1/2\n            //   - If opponent discards studied2 (prob 1/3): same as above -> prob 1/2\n            // Total: 1/3 * 1 + 1/3 * 1/2 + 1/3 * 1/2 = 2/3\n            \n            if (i >= 2 && (n - i) >= 1) {\n                prob += (double) (i * (i - 1) / 2) * (n - i) / total * (2.0/3.0);\n            }\n            \n            // Case 3: Exactly 1 map is from Johnny's studied maps, 2 are not\n            // Number of ways: C(i,1) * C(n-i,2)\n            // Johnny will discard one of the un-studied maps (he wants to keep the studied one)\n            // Opponent picks randomly\n            \n            // If Johnny sees 1 studied + 2 un-studied:\n            // Johnny will discard one un-studied (he has 2 choices, but result is same)\n            // Opponent randomly picks one of 3 to discard\n            //   - If opponent discards studied (prob 1/3): remaining 2 un-studied, system picks randomly -> prob 0\n            //   - If opponent discards un-studied1 (prob 1/3): remaining are studied and un-studied2, system picks randomly -> prob 1/2\n            //   - If opponent discards un-studied2 (prob 1/3): same as above -> prob 1/2\n            // Total: 1/3 * 0 + 1/3 * 1/2 + 1/3 * 1/2 = 1/3\n            \n            if (i >= 1 && (n - i) >= 2) {\n                prob += (double) i * (n - i) * (n - i - 1) / 2 / total * (1.0/3.0);\n            }\n            \n            // Case 4: 0 maps from Johnny's studied maps\n            // Probability: C(n-i,3)/C(n,3) * 0 = 0\n            // So we don't add anything\n            \n            if (prob >= p) {\n                out.println(i);\n                break;\n            }\n        }\n        sc.close();\n        out.close();\n    }\n}\n</repaired_code>\n<confidence>\n0.85\n</confidence>", "token_logprobs": [-1.5497195136049413e-06, -1.537788011773955e-05, -5.483612312673358e-06, 0.0, -3.802703940891661e-05, -1.728519782773219e-05, -3.576278118089249e-07, -4.2437604861333966e-05, -9.536738616588991e-07, -1.3470558769768104e-05, -3.576278118089249e-07, -0.00025459862081333995, -2.3841830625315197e-06, -1.0490362910786644e-05, -0.0008559139096178114, -9.536738616588991e-07, -5.364403477869928e-06, -2.3841830625315197e-06, -0.00043561504571698606, -6.437280717364047e-06, -2.5152843591058627e-05, 0.0, -8.344646857949556e-07, -9.536738616588991e-07, -3.3378546504536644e-06, -9.2982809